\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Definition}
\title{Pretraining and base-model endowment}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{language-model}
\tag{pretraining}
\tag{scaling-law}

\p{A \strong{pretraining protocol} consists of a data law
#{\mathcal D_{\mathrm{pre}}}, an architecture and tokenizer, an objective, an
optimizer, and a resource budget. For next-token prediction the population
objective has the form}

##{
  L_{\mathrm{pre}}(\theta)
  =\mathbb E_{x\sim\mathcal D_{\mathrm{pre}}}
    \left[-\sum_t\log\pi_\theta(x_t\mid x_{<t})\right].
}

\p{Running the protocol produces a \strong{base policy} #{\pi_{\theta_0}}.
Its \strong{base-model endowment} on a task law #{\mathcal Q} is the collection
of properties present before task-specific post-training: probability mass on
successful trajectories, calibration, representation and workspace geometry,
context use, effective compute cost, and the diversity of strategies reachable
by the chosen inference procedure.}

\p{The term “endowment” is intentionally richer than a benchmark score. Two
models can have the same pass-at-one score while assigning very different mass
to rare successful traces, or while requiring very different inference costs
to expose them. Likewise, the same architecture trained on different data or
with a different optimizer need not provide the same starting policy.}
